1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,773
Kokasa rode out into the rugged expanse of the Western Deccan.

2
00:00:04,773 --> 00:00:10,415
He was not looking for beauty; he was looking for raw lithological strength.

3
00:00:10,415 --> 00:00:17,359
Sixty-six million years earlier, at the end of the age of dinosaurs, the earth tore open.

4
00:00:17,359 --> 00:00:21,699
Millions of cubic kilometers of molten lava flooded the region.

5
00:00:21,699 --> 00:00:29,944
Layer upon layer cooled into stacked horizontal sheets of tholeiitic basalt, creating the towering terraces of the Sahyadri mountains.

6
00:00:29,944 --> 00:00:33,416
Kokasa arrived at Elapura, the sacred Charanandri ridge.

7
00:00:33,416 --> 00:00:37,756
A gently sloping escarpment rising thirty meters above the plain.

8
00:00:37,756 --> 00:00:47,304
The location was geographically perfect: it stood adjacent to the sacred Shiva shrine of Ghrishneshwar, and directly guarded the Dakshinapatha trade corridor.

9
00:00:47,304 --> 00:00:52,511
Existing caves had already been carved here by Buddhist and Hindu ascetics.

10
00:00:52,511 --> 00:00:55,115
Kokasa knew the rock held together.

11
00:00:55,115 --> 00:00:58,587
But was it strong enough for a monolith?

12
00:00:58,587 --> 00:01:03,795
He took a bronze testing mallet and struck the upper rock shelf.

13
00:01:03,795 --> 00:01:13,343
If the stone rang with a dull, hollow thud, it was amygdaloidal, full of weak zeolite bubbles that would shatter under stress.

14
00:01:13,343 --> 00:01:17,248
He moved further down the ridge and struck again.

15
00:01:17,248 --> 00:01:23,758
This time, the stone rang out like a temple bell: a clear, high-pitched metallic chime.

16
00:01:23,758 --> 00:01:25,928
This was dense microcrystalline basalt.

17
00:01:25,928 --> 00:01:32,872
Its compressive strength exceeded one hundred and forty megapascals, twice as strong as modern structural concrete.

18
00:01:32,872 --> 00:01:36,778
Kokasa turned to his apprentices: 'Here is our mountain.

19
00:01:36,778 --> 00:01:39,815
It will hold the house of Shiva.'

